# Wikipedia

The knowledge base has a namespace of its own: every being has an id,
`kronos`, `nemean-lion`, `hecatoncheires`. Two external namespaces name the
same beings differently. Wikipedia names them by article title, `Cronus`,
`Nemean lion`, `Hecatoncheires`, and Wikidata by item, `Q44204`. This card
holds the mapping from the first namespace to the other two.

The mapping is the table at the end of this card, not a record file: a
person reads it, follows its links and edits it here. The module
`myth.wikipedia` reads the table back out of the card's text, so the other
documents' cells can join it with the ontology, as [[olympians]] and
[[heracles]] do.

Each title was resolved through Wikipedia's API on 16 November 2026,
following redirects. Each article's description was then read, to keep out
pages that list several figures of one name. Menoetius and Alcaeus have
only such a page, and their rows say so.

```hql id=mapped hidden
import myth.wikipedia

wikipedia.articles(@[[wikipedia]].body.unwrap_or(""))
```

## The namespaces agree

Every being of the knowledge base has a row, and every row names a being.
A check of the table against the ontology, run each time the card is
rendered:

```hql#eval
import myth.kb
import myth.wikipedia

ids = kb.beings() | map(b => b.id)
named = mapped | map(a => a.being)

[
    {check: "beings in the knowledge base", count: ids | size},
    {check: "rows in the table", count: named | size},
    {check: "beings with no row", count: ids | filter(i => not named.contains(i)) | size},
    {check: "rows naming no being", count: named | filter(n => not ids.contains(n)) | size},
    {check: "rows without a Wikidata item", count: mapped | filter(a => a.wikidata == "") | size},
]
| Table(columns = ["check", "count"])
```
<!-- hql:output hash=c07c26d1f105 status=ok -->
| check                        | count |
| ---------------------------- | ----- |
| beings in the knowledge base | 103   |
| rows in the table            | 103   |
| beings with no row           | 0     |
| rows naming no being         | 0     |
| rows without a Wikidata item | 2     |
<!-- /hql:output -->

## By kind

The articles of each kind of being, the way the ontology sorts them and
Wikipedia does not.

```hql#eval
import myth.kb
import myth.beings
import myth.wikipedia

kb.beings()
| group(by = b => b.kind)
| map(g => {kind: g.key, articles: g.items | map(b => wikipedia.article(mapped, b.id)) | somes | map(a => a.link()) | join(", ")})
| Table(columns = ["kind", "articles"])
```
